abstract=r'''\abstract{Streaming large language model (LLM) services can expose response-length metadata even when payloads are encrypted. We evaluate a fixed T5 reconstructor using validated application-layer byte-length traces from 300 aligned prompts for each of seven open-weight models. Complete reconstruction of 2100 archived responses under a 512-token victim budget yields mean term-frequency cosine scores ($\phi$) of 0.482--0.535 for five models. TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini retain malformed archived word boundaries and are analyzed as diagnostics. Separate generation of 300 responses per model with verified full-sequence detokenization yields mean scores of 0.435 and 0.504; these new cohorts are not pooled into the archived comparison. In 96-token pilots, replacing the trace by a constant reduces lexical scores for both tested models, while shuffling is significant only for Qwen after multiplicity correction; cross-prompt contrasts are inconclusive. Five framing transformations reduce frozen-reconstructor mean scores by 36.7--88.7\%. A 3300-reconstruction study with five decoder seeds and a prompt-disjoint maximum-a-posteriori (MAP) length inverse recovers much of the score lost under bucketing and batching. This study decodes the first heuristic segment against the full response; the primary experiment decodes all segments. Input and output limits of 80 T5 tokens make segmentation and retained encoder coverage part of each comparison. The results establish reproducible lexical behavior of this instrumented pipeline, without measuring semantic disclosure, intrinsic model vulnerability, or passive recovery from encrypted transport.}'''
paragraph(r'\abstract{',abstract)
paragraph('This paper separates trace-based reconstruction',r'''This paper separates reconstruction from a known length trace from extraction of that trace from encrypted traffic. The laboratory observable is the ordered UTF-8 byte length of each token fragment emitted by an instrumented service, before transport processing. A validated log oracle supplies these lengths and preserves newline bytes and empty fragments. This abstraction removes segmentation by HTTP and Transport Layer Security (TLS), buffering, retransmission, and multiplexing from the experiment. The historical line-collector discrepancy motivates the validation in Section~\ref{sec:measurement-audit}; all quantitative reconstruction results reported below use recorded, validated input vectors. No experiment here establishes passive extraction of event boundaries from encrypted traffic.''')
paragraph('Building on Weiss',r'''Building on Weiss \emph{et al.}, who reconstructed streamed responses from token-length patterns using a T5-based inversion pipeline~\cite{weiss2024prompt}, we examine one fixed reconstructor across open-weight victim models. Partial trace ablations assess its sensitivity to ordered lengths, and framing transformations test its response without retraining. The seven-model cohort retains archived emitted texts; TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini are diagnostic within that cohort because of malformed word boundaries. A separate two-model experiment uses newly generated responses with verified full-sequence detokenization.''')
replace('How do saved reconstruction scores differ across seven victim-model runs, and which comparisons remain interpretable after auditing collection and reference decoding?', 'How do scores from validated length traces differ across seven archived victim-model cohorts, and how do separate experiments with verified reference decoding inform the two diagnostic series?')
paragraph('The contribution is an artifact-backed evaluation',r'''The contribution is an evaluation with explicit input provenance: 2100 reconstructions from validated archived lengths, 600 additional reconstructions of newly generated and correctly assembled responses, two-model pilots of partial trace ablations and five framing configurations, and a five-seed MAP-inverse evaluation. The pilots and MAP study use different reconstruction scopes, so their baselines are not interchangeable. Full input vectors, decoder inputs, random seeds, generated texts, and independent metric checks accompany the results at \url{https://github.com/jackaugustyn/LLeakM}. Appendix~\ref{app:runs} identifies the completed experiment artifacts and distinguishes them from historical manifests.''')
paragraph('We evaluate a length-only reconstructor',r'''We evaluate a length-only reconstructor for an instrumented LLM service. The observation is the ordered byte length of each emitted token fragment; the attacker receives neither the generating prompt, token identities, nor victim-model internals. The evaluator uses instrumented logs to verify these lengths and to obtain reference text, but supplies only the lengths to the reconstructor. This application-layer oracle does not recover event boundaries from TLS, HTTP/2, or QUIC traffic. The archived-reference cohort and the newly generated two-model cohort are kept separate because verified byte lengths do not themselves establish ordinary detokenization of historical outputs.''')
replace(r'Intended observation:\\ordered payload byte lengths',r'Validated log oracle:\\ordered payload byte lengths')
replace('Intended threat model. The attacker receives application-layer lengths without the prompt or token identities. The collection-path discrepancy is documented in Section~\\ref{sec:measurement-audit}; passive recovery of event boundaries from encrypted transport is outside the experiment.', 'Instrumented threat model. The evaluator verifies logged payload lengths and supplies them to the attacker without the prompt or token identities. Passive recovery of event boundaries from encrypted transport is outside the experiment.')
paragraph('The historical harness emitted',r'''The instrumented harness associates each generated token position with a text fragment and records its UTF-8 length, serialized-write length, and relative timestamp. The historical implementation decoded each token independently; the new-generation implementation uses contextual prefix decoding and verifies that the fragments concatenate exactly to the full decoded token sequence. Each write has the SSE-like serialization \texttt{data: \textless fragment\textgreater\textbackslash n\textbackslash n}, and termination uses \texttt{data: [DONE]\textbackslash n\textbackslash n}. Raw newlines inside fragment text are not escaped, so a write is not necessarily a valid single SSE data event. The present experiments use validated lengths from the instrumented logs, not a line-based parser.''')
paragraph('where the eight bytes are the prefix',r'''where the eight bytes are the prefix \texttt{data: } and suffix \texttt{\textbackslash n\textbackslash n}. This identity concerns the complete emitted byte string, including newline bytes inside the fragment. The terminal \texttt{[DONE]} write is excluded. Validation checks per-fragment byte counts, their order, concatenated reference text, total payload bytes, and sample/log identity. For newly collected streams the collector also checks the total received byte count. Exact vectors, source hashes, and reference text are saved with every reconstruction input. The primary, control, defense, and MAP experiments all use this explicit log-length representation.''')
block(r'\subsection{Measurement and Reference-Text Audit}',r'\subsection{Reconstruction Pipeline}',r'''\subsection{Measurement and Reference-Text Audit}\label{sec:measurement-audit}
The archived collector used \texttt{requests.iter\_lines()}, discarded empty lines, and appended two newline bytes to each remaining line. Its inferred vector $X^{\mathrm{col}}$ can differ from the logged vector $X^{\mathrm{log}}$: a fragment \texttt{:\textbackslash n\textbackslash n} contains three bytes, but this parser infers one. Replaying that implementation on the retained logs changes 2073 of 2100 vectors and the heuristic-segment count in 764 samples. These are preprocessing comparisons; the exact historical T5 input vectors and their binding to the old collector version were not retained.

The primary results below instead come from complete T5 inference on the 2100 validated logged vectors. This establishes the inputs used for the reported reconstructions without recovering undocumented historical inputs. Differences from older scores cannot be attributed solely to collection: the execution environment and reconstruction random-state handling also differ. The reconstructor is seeded after checkpoint loading so that cache initialization does not change a sample's random stream.

Reference text requires a separate check. The historical harness's independent token decoding produced no ASCII spaces in 299/300 TinyLlama responses and 162/300 Phi-3.5-mini responses. Those 600 archived references remain diagnostic; validating their emitted byte lengths does not restore their word boundaries. Historical token IDs are unavailable for all seven models, so complete-sequence detokenization of the other archived series is also not certified.

To evaluate the affected models with ordinary reference text, we generated 300 additional responses per model from the same prompts using contextual detokenization. All 600 passed exact agreement between concatenated fragments and full-sequence decoding, with token IDs, tokenizer and checkpoint provenance retained; all contain ASCII spaces. These new cohorts are reported separately. Current pinned checkpoint revisions, precision and placement are documented, but their identity with the historical execution is not established; the new results do not isolate a causal detokenization effect.''')
paragraph('Victim-model decoding was deterministic',r'''Victim-model decoding used greedy selection, with temperature 0.0 and \texttt{top\_p}=1.0. The primary cohort contains $N=300$ archived responses per model generated with \texttt{max\_new\_tokens}=512. Each reconstruction requests all heuristic segments (\texttt{max\_sentences}=0), samples three candidates per segment, and retains up to three first-segment candidates. Six historical victim models used a one-token KV-cache loop, while Gemma-2-2B-it used greedy Hugging Face \texttt{model.generate()}. Responses reaching EOS before the limit are marked \texttt{response\_complete}; length-truncated responses remain in the primary analysis. EOS-complete subsets are descriptive subsets, not interchangeable samples.''')
paragraph('The evaluated models are listed',r'''The evaluated models are listed in Table~\ref{tab:model_metadata}. Six are instruction- or chat-tuned; Phi-1.5 is a base-model stress test and does not identify a general base-versus-instruction effect~\cite{qwen25report,llama3report,llama32blog,gemma2report,phi15report,tinyllama2024,phi3report}. A common token budget does not imply a common text length across tokenizers. Table~\ref{tab:length_covariates} describes the 2100 archived emitted responses, which are unchanged by reconstruction. Their truncation rates range from 34.3\% to 100\%; archived Phi-3.5-mini has no EOS-complete response.

The additional TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini generations use the same 300 prompts, the legacy prompt format, greedy decoding and a 512-token limit. TinyLlama revision \texttt{fe8a4ea1ffedaf415f4da2f062534de366a451e6} is loaded in float16 on CUDA; Phi-3.5-mini revision \texttt{2fe192450127e6a83f7441aef6e3ca586c338b77} uses float16 with automatic CPU offload to fit the available memory. Reconstruction for every cohort uses float32 T5 checkpoints on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3070 Ti Laptop GPU with 8 GB VRAM, PyTorch 2.5.1 and Transformers 4.46.3. Exact model files, revisions and environment details are bound to each protocol in the artifacts.''')
replace('Victim models in the retained campaign. TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini are apparatus diagnostics because of unresolved reference detokenization. Parameter counts follow the cited model cards and technical reports.', 'Victim models in the archived-reference cohort. TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini are diagnostic in that cohort and also evaluated separately with newly generated, validated references. Parameter counts follow the cited model cards and technical reports.')
paragraph('Reconstruction quality was computed',r'''Reconstruction quality was computed against the complete reference response $R$ using four lexical criteria. The normalized Levenshtein value is $\texttt{ed\_norm}=d(R,\widehat{R})/|R|$, using reference character length as the denominator; it can exceed 1 when the prediction is much longer than the reference~\cite{levenshtein1966binary}. ROUGE-1 precision and ROUGE-L precision measure unigram and longest-common-subsequence overlap~\cite{lin2004rouge}. The primary metric $\phi$ is cosine similarity between unweighted, case-normalized term-frequency vectors~\cite{salton1975vector}, with terms defined by \texttt{[A-Za-z0-9']+}. All reported metrics were recomputed from complete saved text, using independent edit-distance and subsequence implementations checked against dynamic programming. The MAP experiment scores the first heuristic segment's prediction against the entire response, not against an annotated first sentence.''')
replace('The documented pilot recipe reconstructs up to three heuristic segments against the full stored response, although the archived pilot rows do not retain per-sample segment counts.', 'The control experiment reconstructs up to three heuristic segments against the full stored response and saves the actual segment counts and encoder inputs.')
paragraph('Pairwise differences in per-prompt',r'''Pairwise differences in per-prompt $\phi$ were assessed using two-sided paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests~\cite{wilcoxon1945individual}. Absolute nonzero differences receive average ranks for ties; zero differences are discarded. The normal approximation uses a tie-adjusted variance and a continuity correction of 0.5, with normal tails evaluated directly to avoid numerical cancellation. Holm--Bonferroni correction is applied separately to 21 model comparisons, six control--true-trace comparisons and ten defense--baseline comparisons~\cite{holm1979simple}; rank-biserial correlation summarizes effect size. Significance denotes adjusted $p<0.05$. A bootstrap interval for a mean difference and a multiplicity-adjusted signed-rank test address different summaries and are not treated as interchangeable.''')
paragraph('The control and defense pilots derive',r'''The control and defense experiments use explicit BLAKE2s-derived, condition-specific decoder seeds and record every seed and transformed vector. Shuffling uses seed $20260706+17i$ for prompt index $i$. Cross-prompt inputs cycle the trace of donor index $(i\bmod30)+1$ to the target's trace length. Random padding has a separately recorded transformation seed, and its payload overhead is calculated from the actual saved padding realization. These are newly specified pilot random draws, not recovered historical seeds. The control and defense baseline reconstructions use distinct decoder seeds, so even their identical undefended length traces can yield different mean scores. Historical \texttt{hash()}-dependent seeds and inconsistent padding-cost summaries are excluded from the current results.''')
replace('The documented pilot recipe reconstructs up to three heuristic segments and scores their concatenation against the full stored response.', 'The pilot reconstructs up to three heuristic segments, with three samples per segment, and scores their concatenation against the full stored response.')
replace('A run is admitted to the manuscript table only when \\texttt{robustness\\_eval.json} reports \\texttt{complete: true}.', 'All 22 cells contain their full 30-prompt by five-seed design. Source vectors, inverse inputs, and decoder seeds are retained; the complete experiment contains 3300 reconstructions.')
paragraph('A replay of input preparation',r'''The recorded encoder inputs quantify first-segment coverage (Table~\ref{tab:encoder_coverage}). Baseline first segments have median lengths of 14.5 observed units for Qwen and 16 for Llama, all retained. For \texttt{bucket\_8}, median segments contain 451.5 and 441.5 units, but only the first 64 units fit after the instruction prefix. For \texttt{pad\_32}, 32 units fit because each length value occupies two T5 tokens. With MAP inversion, all 30 unique inputs per model remain truncated for bucketing and fixed padding; counts are one per model for \texttt{batch\_2}, zero for \texttt{batch\_4}, and eight (Qwen) and two (Llama) for \texttt{rand\_pad\_8}. Five decoder seeds reuse each condition's input. Actual saved token IDs were checked against the pinned tokenizer. The comparisons therefore combine changes in length values, segmentation, encoded representation, and retained prefix; observation coverage is not held constant.''')
replace('First-segment encoder inputs exceeding 80 T5 tokens in preprocessing replay of the frozen MAP-study conditions (30 test prompts per model). The limit includes the instruction prefix. This table audits input coverage, not reconstruction quality.', 'Recorded first-segment encoder inputs exceeding 80 T5 tokens in the frozen MAP conditions (30 unique test inputs per model, each decoded with five seeds). The limit includes the instruction prefix. Counts describe encoder coverage, not reconstruction quality.')
paragraph('All seven archived runs used',r'''The completed experiments comprise 2100 reconstructions from validated archived length traces, 600 reconstructions of newly generated TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini responses, 240 trace-control reconstructions, 360 defense-pilot reconstructions and 3300 MAP reconstructions. Every planned sample is present and independently checked. The primary and new-reference cohorts reconstruct all available heuristic segments; the 96-token pilots allow up to three, and the 512-token MAP experiment reconstructs one. These differences preclude interpreting cross-experiment score differences as victim-budget effects alone.''')
paragraph('Table~\\ref{tab:trace_controls} shows',r'''Table~\ref{tab:trace_controls} shows that the effect of removing trace features depends on both model and control. For Qwen, the true-trace mean is 0.2904 versus 0.2084 after shuffling and 0.1169 for a constant trace; both contrasts are significant after Holm correction ($p=0.0053$ and $p<0.0001$). For Llama, the true mean is 0.2221 versus 0.1272 for a constant trace ($p=0.0010$). Its shuffled mean is lower (0.1798), but the adjusted signed-rank test is inconclusive ($p=0.2634$). Cross-prompt means are 0.2522 for Qwen and 0.2250 for Llama; neither contrast is significant ($p=0.3625$ and $p=0.8531$). Thus these pilots do not establish a general advantage over unrelated prompt traces. Each control retains some response-dependent information, and transformations can also alter segmentation and encoder coverage.''')
replace('The documented recipe decodes up to three heuristic segments, with 80-token T5 input and output limits, and scores their concatenation against the full stored response.', 'Each run decodes up to three heuristic segments with three samples per segment, 80-token T5 input and output limits, and the full stored response as reference.')
block(r'\subsection{Archived Cross-Model Scores and Reference-Format Diagnostics}',r'\begin{table*}[ht]',r'''\subsection{Reconstruction from Validated Archived Lengths}
Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports the completed reconstruction cohort, and Figure~\ref{fig:model_ci} shows its mean-score intervals. For the five series without the observed malformed-word-boundary pattern, mean $\phi$ ranges from 0.4820 to 0.5355 and $\mathrm{LR}@0.5$ from 42.33\% to 65.33\%. Their ROUGE-1 precision is 0.2101--0.2921. These metrics quantify lexical overlap with the archived emitted text; they do not establish semantic fidelity or independently validate its historical full-sequence detokenization. Phi-1.5 remains a base-model stress test.

The archived TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini references yield means of 0.0792 and 0.1210, with no $\phi>0.5$ cases. These are reference-format diagnostics. The new input vectors are verified byte-for-byte for all models, but reference defects remain part of these two scores. Across the 2100 reconstructions, all 62,757 available heuristic segments were processed; 1475 encoder calls exceeded the 80-token input limit. No run stopped early because of an empty middle-segment output.''')
replace('Archived cross-model scores recomputed with one term-frequency cosine implementation ($N=300$ per model; 512-token victim budget; all available heuristic segments; 80-token T5 input and output limits per segment). Reconstructions are scored against full stored responses. The final two rows are reference-format diagnostics; historical collector inputs remain unverified for all rows.', 'Complete reconstructions from validated archived payload lengths ($N=300$ per model; 512-token victim budget; all available heuristic segments; three samples per segment; 80-token T5 input and output limits). Full archived emitted responses are the references. The final two rows are reference-format diagnostics; input provenance is verified for every row.')
replace('Archived mean lexical scores and bootstrap 95\\% confidence intervals ($N=300$ per model; 512-token victim budget; all available heuristic segments scored against full responses). Each segment uses 80-token T5 input and output limits. $\\dagger$ marks reference-format diagnostics, which do not support transfer claims. All rows retain unverified collector provenance. Error-bar half-widths are rounded; asymmetric endpoints appear in Table~\\ref{tab:statistical_results}.', 'Mean lexical scores from validated archived lengths with prompt-bootstrap 95\\% confidence intervals ($N=300$ per model; all heuristic segments scored against full archived references). Each segment uses three samples and 80-token T5 input and output limits. $\\dagger$ marks reference-format diagnostics. Rounded error bars display the asymmetric endpoints in Table~\\ref{tab:statistical_results}.')
paragraph('Primary-campaign $\\phi$ values',r'''Relative to the earlier stored reconstructions, mean $\phi$ changes by $-0.020$ to $-0.035$ for the five non-diagnostic series, while the diagnostic TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini means increase. This is a comparison of reconstruction executions on the same archived references, not an isolated estimate of a collector effect. The reported primary metrics use complete new predictions and a single lexical scoring implementation.''')
paragraph('Table~\\ref{tab:eos_completeness} compares',r'''Table~\ref{tab:eos_completeness} compares all 300 archived responses per model with the subset whose victim generation reached EOS. The generation-completeness rates are unchanged because reconstruction uses the same references. EOS restriction does not systematically increase $\phi$: Qwen2.5-3B rises from 0.5251 to 0.5551 ($n=50$), whereas Phi-1.5 falls from 0.5164 to 0.4868 ($n=144$). Phi-3.5-mini has no EOS-complete subset. EOS indicates completion of victim generation, not correct reconstruction; conditioning on it also changes the evaluated response subset.''')
replace('Victim-response EOS completeness in the 512-token-budget campaign ($N=300$ per model). All available heuristic segments are reconstructed with 80-token T5 input and output limits and scored against full stored responses. EOS-only metrics select \\texttt{response\\_complete} samples; they do not certify complete reconstructions. Phi-3.5-mini and TinyLlama remain reference-format diagnostics, and collector provenance remains unverified.', 'EOS subsets of the archived-reference cohort ($N=300$ per model; validated length inputs, all segments, 80-token T5 input and output limits). EOS-only metrics select \\texttt{response\\_complete} samples; they do not certify correct reconstructions. Phi-3.5-mini and TinyLlama remain reference-format diagnostics.')
block(r'\subsection{Statistical Uncertainty and Pairwise Tests}',r'\begin{table*}[ht]',r'''\subsection{Statistical Uncertainty and Pairwise Tests}
Table~\ref{tab:statistical_results} gives prompt-bootstrap intervals and Wilson intervals for the lexical threshold. Fifteen of 21 model pairs are significant after Holm correction, with the same decisions when the 60 scenario means are used as the paired units. Among Qwen2.5-1.5B, Qwen2.5-3B, Llama-3.2-3B and Phi-1.5, all six pairwise tests are inconclusive after correction. The significant family also contains comparisons with malformed-reference diagnostics, which cannot be interpreted as transfer differences on ordinary model responses.

For example, Qwen2.5-1.5B minus Qwen2.5-3B has a mean difference of 0.0103 (prompt-bootstrap 95\% CI $[-0.0031,0.0232]$, rank-biserial $r=0.1057$, $p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=0.4500$). Qwen2.5-1.5B minus Llama has a mean difference of 0.0208 (95\% CI $[0.0059,0.0359]$, $r=0.1408$), but its multiplicity-adjusted signed-rank test is inconclusive ($p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=0.2076$). These results illustrate why unadjusted mean-difference intervals and adjusted rank tests should be distinguished.''')
replace('Intervals for the archived 512-token-budget scores ($N=300$ per model; all available heuristic segments, each with 80-token T5 input and output limits, scored against full responses). Mean-$\\phi$ intervals use 10,000 prompt-index bootstrap resamples; $\\mathrm{LR}@0.5$ intervals are Wilson 95\\% intervals. These intervals do not cover measurement errors or shared-scenario dependence. Phi-3.5-mini and TinyLlama are reference-format diagnostics.', 'Intervals for reconstructions from validated archived lengths ($N=300$ per model; all segments, 80-token T5 input and output limits). Mean-$\\phi$ intervals use 10,000 prompt-index bootstrap resamples; $\\mathrm{LR}@0.5$ intervals are Wilson 95\\% intervals. These nominal intervals do not include shared-scenario dependence or reference-format error. The final two rows remain diagnostic.')
paragraph('An exploratory analysis using scenarios',r'''The exploratory scenario analysis retains the same 15 significant pairs (Table~\ref{tab:scenario_sensitivity}). All seven scenario-bootstrap intervals are wider than their prompt-bootstrap counterparts. For the Qwen2.5-1.5B versus Qwen2.5-3B comparison, the mean difference remains 0.0103, with scenario-level $r=0.2470$ and $p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=0.3876$. Grouping the five variants changes the inference unit; agreement of significance decisions does not establish independence or generalization to unseen scenarios.''')
replace('Exploratory scenario-level sensitivity of the archived scores: five prompt variants averaged within each of 60 scenarios per model, with 10,000 scenario-bootstrap resamples. These intervals concern the corpus mean and retain the collection and reference limitations. $\\dagger$ denotes a reference-format diagnostic.', 'Exploratory scenario-level sensitivity of the validated-length reconstructions: five prompt variants averaged within each of 60 scenarios per model, with 10,000 scenario-bootstrap resamples. These intervals concern the corpus mean; $\\dagger$ denotes a reference-format diagnostic.')
block(r'\subsection{What the Lexical Scores Represent}',r'\subsection{Effects of Framing Transformations',r'''\subsection{Separate Experiments with Verified Reference Decoding}\label{sec:fresh}
New TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini generations passed full-sequence detokenization checks for all 300 prompts per model. Their corresponding mean scores are 0.4352 and 0.5042 (Table~\ref{tab:fresh_results}), with $\mathrm{LR}@0.5$ of 30.33\% and 52.33\%. TinyLlama reached EOS in 177/300 responses; all Phi-3.5-mini responses reached the 512-token limit. All 8835 and 10,748 available reconstruction segments were processed, with 55 and 77 encoder calls truncated, respectively.

These cohorts show that the very low malformed-reference scores do not characterize reconstruction from normally assembled responses under the new configurations. They are not pooled with the five other archived-reference series into a new seven-model ranking. Historical victim revision identity is unknown, and generation execution and reference construction changed together; the differences do not isolate the effect of detokenization alone.''')
paragraph('All five transformations reduced the mean',r'''All five framing transformations reduce mean $\phi$ relative to their defense-pilot baseline (Table~\ref{tab:defense_results}), and all ten paired comparisons are significant after Holm correction. Reductions range from 36.7\% for \texttt{pad\_32} on Qwen to 88.7\% for \texttt{bucket\_8} on Qwen. Batching reduces scores by 67.2--73.2\% and changes the number of observable units from 96 to 48 or 24. Figure~\ref{fig:defense_tradeoff} relates these changes to payload overhead calculated from the actual saved transformation vectors.''')
replace('Framing transformations on logged lengths from the 96-token pilot ($n=30$ paired prompts per model). The documented frozen-reconstructor recipe decodes up to three heuristic segments with 80-token T5 input and output limits and scores their concatenation against the full stored response. Overhead includes only protected token-payload units, excluding SSE syntax, transport headers, buffering delay, and latency. $^{*}$For randomized padding, the analytically expected overhead replaces unrecoverable historical draw costs; lexical scores remain those of the archived runs.', 'Framing transformations on validated lengths from 96-token responses ($n=30$ paired prompts per model; three samples per segment, up to three segments, 80-token T5 input and output limits, full-response references). Every overhead, including randomized padding, is the realized cost of the saved transformed vectors. Payload accounting excludes SSE syntax, transport headers, buffering delay and latency.')
replace('Recovery-score reduction versus protected-payload overhead, averaged over the two 96-token pilot models ($n=30$ prompts each; documented recipe: up to three heuristic segments, 80-token T5 input and output limits, full-response reference). The randomized-padding point ($^{*}$) combines expected overhead (81.4\\%) with the archived score reduction; it is not a joint measurement of a recovered padding realization. Batching has zero payload-sum overhead here and changes the frame count; latency was not measured. The plot describes the frozen pipeline, including its segment and input-coverage changes, rather than information-theoretic privacy.', 'Recovery-score reduction versus realized protected-payload overhead, averaged equally over the two 96-token pilot models ($n=30$ prompts each; up to three segments, three samples per segment, 80-token T5 input and output limits). Both coordinates use the completed runs. Batching has zero payload-sum overhead and changes the frame count; latency was not measured. These are pipeline scores, not information-theoretic privacy estimates.')
paragraph('The partial trace ablations show',r'''The strongest control evidence concerns replacing the trace by a constant: both models lose lexical score, and shuffling also reduces Qwen's score after correction. Neither cross-prompt contrast is conclusive, and Llama's shuffled contrast is inconclusive after Holm correction. Thus the experiments support sensitivity to selected trace features, not a universal advantage of the target trace over every partial ablation. Controls preserve response-dependent quantities such as length, mean or histogram and can change segmentation. Their residual scores cannot be assigned entirely to the decoder's language prior. Estimating information-theoretic leakage would require a defined response variable, repeated sampling and a suitable estimator.''')
block(r'\subsection{Limits of the Cross-Model Comparison}',r'\subsection{Practical Exploitability}',r'''\subsection{Limits of the Cross-Model Comparison}
Exact logged byte lengths remove the line-collector ambiguity from the reported reconstruction inputs. This matters because a correct arithmetic reanalysis alone would leave the observation layer unresolved. Reference quality remains a distinct issue: archived TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini fragments have malformed word boundaries, whereas the new two-model cohorts pass explicit full-sequence decoding checks. Their substantially higher scores make the archived diagnostics unsuitable as general evidence of poor transfer.

The five other archived-reference series support conditional comparisons of the fixed reconstructor on their emitted texts, not an intrinsic model-vulnerability ranking. Historical token IDs and exact victim revisions are unavailable, while tokenizer vocabulary, response style, refusal behavior and compatibility with T5 training data can differ. The fresh cohorts are consequently presented separately. The synthetic corpus also contains only 60 scenarios, and the MAP test and calibration sets share those scenarios despite exact-prompt disjointness.''')
paragraph('For the largest recorded mean',r'''For the largest mean in the archived-reference cohort, $\mathrm{LR}@0.5$ is 65.33\% and ROUGE-1 precision is 0.2921. Neither quantity is a disclosure rate: lexical overlap does not verify an entity, proposition or actionable answer. No semantic annotation or task-specific disclosure evaluation was performed. Practical harm might involve recovered phrases, entities, refusals or topic cues, but the present metrics do not quantify those outcomes. Deployment-facing conclusions additionally require extraction of observables from encrypted traffic and validation under transport buffering and multiplexing.''')
block(r'\subsection{Limitations}',r'\subsection{Ethical Considerations}',r'''\subsection{Limitations}
The observation is an instrumented log oracle. No packet capture or experiment with TLS, HTTP/2 or QUIC demonstrates that its token boundaries are available to a passive observer. The two historical reference-format diagnostics remain in the archived cohort for traceability, and the other archived references lack original token-ID provenance. Newly generated references are verified for TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini, but their configurations are not certified replicas of the historical victim executions.

The primary cohorts use one assembled reconstruction per prompt with three stochastic samples per segment. Decoder-seed uncertainty is evaluated separately for two models and 30 prompts; five seeds are not five independent prompt samples. Scenario bootstrap accounts for grouping within the 60 represented scenarios, without establishing generalization beyond the synthetic corpus. MAP calibration and test inputs are disjoint as exact prompt strings, not as scenarios. Pilot decoder seeds are condition-specific, and their single realizations cannot separate all stochastic variation from transformation effects.

The main archived responses reach the token limit in 34.3--100\% of cases; both historical and new Phi-3.5-mini cohorts lack EOS-complete responses. Reconstruction processes all heuristic segments in the primary and new-reference cohorts, up to three in the 96-token pilots, and one in MAP. All use the full stored response as the scoring reference. Transform-dependent segmentation and the 80-token encoder limit prevent treating these scopes as matched information budgets. The MAP inverse is not end-to-end retraining, and payload overhead excludes transport syntax, buffering delay and latency. Finally, the historical Gemma cohort uses a different victim-generation implementation from the other six archived cohorts.''')
block(r'\section{Conclusions}',r'\appendixtitles',r'''\section{Conclusions}
Complete inference from validated logged token lengths yields reproducible lexical scores for an instrumented T5 reconstruction pipeline. Five archived-reference series have means of 0.482--0.535; two additional archived series remain diagnostics because of malformed word boundaries. Separate experiments with verified detokenization yield mean scores of 0.435 for TinyLlama and 0.504 for Phi-3.5-mini. They strengthen the need to distinguish observation validity from reference validity and are not combined into an intrinsic seven-model vulnerability ranking.

The 96-token controls support sensitivity to selected trace features, with three of six contrasts significant after Holm correction and neither cross-prompt contrast conclusive. Five framing transformations reduce the frozen reconstructor's mean score by 36.7--88.7\%. In the first-segment, five-seed experiment, a prompt-disjoint MAP length inverse recovers much of the loss under bucketing and batching. These are effects of the full preprocessing and decoding pipeline, including unequal retained encoder coverage. Tests with fixed observation coverage, unseen scenarios, encrypted transport and disclosure-oriented outcomes are required to extend the findings to practical privacy claims.''')
block(r'\section{Artifact Completeness and Standardized Run Directories}',r'\begin{table}[ht]',r'''\section{Artifact Provenance and Completed Experiments}\label{app:runs}
The artifact repository at \url{https://github.com/jackaugustyn/LLeakM} separates archived victim responses from the completed reconstruction runs. Table~\ref{tab:run_ids} identifies the original 512-token source directories. Current results reside under \path{experiment_validation/revalidation_20261031/}: \path{inference/cuda_full/} contains the 2100 reconstructions from canonical archived lengths, \path{secondary/cuda_full/} the 3900 control, defense and MAP records, and \path{inference/fresh_tinyllama_cuda/} and \path{inference/fresh_phi_cuda/} the two 300-response new-reference reconstructions. Their generation artifacts are under \path{fresh_references/}.

Each completed run binds its sample inventory, source hashes, model files, code, seeds and environment to a protocol. Independent checks verify complete unique keys, saved text metrics, encoder input tokenization, segment coverage and cohort counts. All planned reconstructions are present; partial probes are excluded. The \path{inference_validation/} reports and the secondary summary contain the numerical sources for the manuscript, while \path{final_review_20261101/} records the final numerical and manuscript checks.

The earlier \path{analysis/repro_manifest.json} and \path{analysis/revision_snapshot_20261031.json} remain historical inventories and are not attestations of the new results. \path{completed_manifest.json} records the completed experiment artifacts before their integration into the manuscript; a subsequent manuscript snapshot has a separate role. These provenance records cannot retroactively establish historical victim revisions or undocumented old reconstruction inputs.''')
replace('Archived run directories for the 512-token campaign ($n=300$ per model). TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini are diagnostic series because of malformed reference word boundaries; all seven runs retain the collection-provenance limitation.', 'Source-response directories for the archived-reference cohort ($n=300$ per model). New reconstructions use validated log lengths; TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini remain reference-format diagnostics in this cohort.')
replace(next(line for line in s.splitlines() if line.startswith(r'\dataavailability{')),r'''\dataavailability{Code, synthetic prompts, archived source responses, completed reconstructions, new-generation provenance and statistical checks are organized in \texttt{experiment\_validation/} at \url{https://github.com/jackaugustyn/LLeakM}. The completed experiment artifacts are under \path{experiment_validation/revalidation_20261031/}. Appendix~\ref{app:runs} identifies the source and result directories and distinguishes current experiment records from historical manifests.}''')

